  "raw": " It is a good book for both kids and adults.\n\nI’d also love to have a copy of this as a Christmas gift. For the more hardnosed, the word processing skills are not bad. The cover will help the geezer who is going to be using the book. Of the eight chapters, in which each has a photo of an Australian wicketkeeper, only one is a serious cricket article about Smith, Warne and the modern game. The rest focus on the two wicketkeeper icons from the game’s past: Bradman and Gillespie, two of those two-handed big blokes who could dominate a game with their feet.\n\nI have no objection to anything they wrote about these two and their impact on the game. Indeed, Bradman and Gillespie are much better known for their feats off the pitch than their on it, which also is a valid and admirable observation. In fact that is kind of the point of this book:",
  "serialized": "It is a good book for both kids and adults. I’d also love to have a copy of this as a Christmas gift. For the more hardnosed, the word processing skills are not bad. The cover will help the geezer who is going to be using the book. Of the eight chapters, in which each has a photo of an Australian wicketkeeper, only one is a serious cricket article about Smith, Warne and the modern game. The rest focus on the two wicketkeeper icons from the game’s past: Bradman and Gillespie, two of those two-handed big blokes who could dominate a game with their feet. I have no objection to anything they wrote about these two and their impact on the game. Indeed, Bradman and Gillespie are much better known for their feats off the pitch than their on it, which also is a valid and admirable observation. In fact that is kind of the point of this book:",
